      "url": "https://www.last.fm/music/Creedence+Clearwater+Revival/Creedence+Clearwater+Revival",
      "wiki_content": "Creedence Clearwater Revival is the first album by the American rock band Creedence Clearwater Revival, released in 1968.\n\nThe reissue liner notes state that while \"Suzie Q\" proved to be a hit, the band had played for years as the Golliwogs in the early 1960s, releasing numerous singles before achieving success in the pop world. While the band did gain success with their chart debut, critics initially denied the band any respect. <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Creedence+Clearwater+Revival/Creedence+Clearwater+Revival\">Read more on Last.fm</a>. User-contributed text is available under the Creative Commons By-SA License; additional terms may apply.",
      "wiki_summary": "Creedence Clearwater Revival is the first album by the American rock band Creedence Clearwater Revival, released in 1968. The reissue liner notes state that while \"Suzie Q\" proved to be a hit, the band had played for years as the Golliwogs in the early 1960s, releasing numerous singles before achieving success in the pop world. While the band did gain success with their chart debut, critics initially denied the band any respect. <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Creedence+Clearwater+Revival/Creedence+Clearwater+Revival\">Read more on Last.fm</a>."
    },
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Creedence Clearwater Revival’s self-titled debut arrived in 1968 and announced the band’s signature blend of swampy rock, blues grit, and rootsy Americana with remarkable confidence. Released on Fantasy Records, it frames John Fogerty’s weathered vocals and lean guitar work around compact, hard-driving songs that already sound fully formed, even as the band was still establishing its identity.\n\nThe album is best known for its powerful opening statement and for introducing the group’s breakthrough cover of “Suzie Q,” a long, urgent workout that helped define their early sound. Although it was not a major chart smash at the time, the record has been widely admired for its stripped-down intensity and for setting the template that Creedence would refine on later albums: direct, hooky, and steeped in American vernacular rock.",
